  "worked_solution": "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=\\frac{9}{2}\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}$ into the formula, we can solve for $r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{9\\pi \\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{27}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the surface area of ​​the sphere can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
